    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, i am studying english phonetics and im a bit confused. can u explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced alveolar fricatives? like what is actually happening with the vocal cords for the /s/ and /z/ sounds?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the English language, the sounds /s/ and /z/ are both alveolar fricatives, but they differ in their voicing. The voiced /z/ sound is produced when the vocal cords vibrate during articulation, releasing air through the constricted region between the tongue and alveolar ridge. In contrast, the unvoiced /s/ sound occurs when the vocal cords are not vibrating, allowing air to escape without any vocal cord vibration. This results in a distinct difference in the resonance and timbre of the two sounds.",
      "source": "target",
